= The present question

The problem is not to attach another visualization, statistic, or classifier
to prime data.  It is to define how an arithmetic ecology changes when:

- several receivers participate in one event but cannot necessarily vary
  independently;
- their boundaries move, intersect, split, or lose rank;
- a prime place becomes an active axis;
- a formulation changes while its returned invariant remains fixed; and
- the order of these changes may or may not matter.

The existing work already supplied each ingredient separately.  Receiver
atlases retained co-present perspectives.  Prime observations retained exact
wheel refinements and valuation lineages.  The formulation atlas retained
nonidentical paths to $pi$ and $e$.  Differential geometry supplied
connections, discriminants, and holonomy.  What remained absent was a common
carrier in which those are directions of one dynamic system.

This paper supplies that carrier.  It changes no Soma source and performs no
new machine experiment.

